# Design Patterns For Privacy Layer SDK

Apply these patterns when adding SDK orchestration, adapters, client factories, or execution flow in this repository.

## Composition

Compose behavior from adapters instead of putting network logic inside the core client.

```ts
const client = new AdapterPrivacyClient({
  wallet,
  storage,
  network,
  policy,
});
```

Use composition when a client is built from interchangeable dependencies and you want thin classes with explicit boundaries.

## Observer

Publish stable progress events without exposing low-level network details in the public stage names.

```ts
await operation.execute({
  onEvent(event) {
    if (event.stage === 'submission' && event.status === 'begin') {
      renderProgress(event);
    }
  },
});
```

Use the observer pattern when execution has multiple stages and UI or logging needs incremental updates.

## Visitor

Route operation-specific behavior through dedicated handlers instead of repeated `switch (kind)` blocks.

```ts
const handlers = {
  deposit: (intent) => policy.validateDeposit(intent),
  transfer: (intent) => policy.validateTransfer(intent),
  withdraw: (intent) => policy.validateWithdraw(intent),
};
```

Use a visitor-style handler map when each operation kind needs different validation or transformation logic.

## Factory

Create configured clients from caller-provided adapters and assets.

```ts
export async function createStellarPrivacyClient(config: StellarPrivacyClientConfig) {
  const resolved = await resolveStellarPrivacyClientConfig(config, createDefaultTransactEngine);
  return createStellarPrivacyClientFromResolvedConfig(resolved.config);
}
```

Use a factory when construction requires validation and assembly steps callers should not repeat.

## Abstract Factory

Provide environment-specific factories that share orchestration but use different asset loading strategies.

```ts
export async function createStellarPrivacyClientFromNodeConfig(config: StellarPrivacyClientConfig) {
  return resolveWithAssets(config, loadNodeAssets);
}
```

Use an abstract factory when browser and Node builds must expose different creation paths without duplicating client logic.
